================================================================================
WORKFLOW EXECUTION FLOW (CORRECTED)
================================================================================

Scheduler → Dedicated Executor → Steep Workflow → run_hpo.py → Runner → Workers

Step-by-step:
1. Scheduler creates dedicated executor instance (g4dn.2xlarge)
2. Executor starts, listens on port 8089
3. Scheduler sends workflow to executor:8089
4. Executor spawns thread, calls executeWorkflowHPO()
5. executeWorkflowHPO() creates Steep_Workflow object ✅ 
6. workflow.execute(hosts) → BLOCKS ✅
7. Steep engine parses YAML, finds service path
8. Steep calls: subprocess.run([python, run_hpo.py, args])
9. run_hpo.py checks hosts: 'on-prem' in hosts? ✅
   - Yes → PlclRunnerHPO (Slurm)
   - No → CloudRunnerHPO (SSH + Ray)
10. Runner sets up Ray on WORKERS (not executor) ✅
11. Training completes, results return through call stack
12. Thread sends completion to scheduler
13. Thread cleans up worker instances
14. Thread exits automatically ✅

================================================================================
THREADING MODEL (CLARIFIED)
================================================================================

Executor Process (Single, always running):
├── Main Thread: HTTP Server (port 8089, always alive)
├── Daemon Thread: Queue Listener (always alive)
├── Workflow Thread 1: executeWorkflowHPO(wf1) → BLOCKS → EXITS when done ✅
├── Workflow Thread 2: executeWorkflowHPO(wf2) → BLOCKS → EXITS when done ✅
└── Workflow Thread 3: executeWorkflowHPO(wf3) → BLOCKS → EXITS when done ✅

Key Properties:
- ✅ Threads are NOT daemon (must complete)
- ✅ No .join() calls (fire-and-forget)
- ✅ Each thread independent (own Steep_Workflow object)
- ✅ Thread exits when function returns (Python GC cleans up)
- ✅ Same model as SeisSol executor

